Jukka Lahtinen wrote:
 > Kent Paul Dolan <xanthian@well.com> writes:

 >> Otherwise, daggers just plain won't stack, and
 >> there's no cure for it, if they differ in:

 >> * individual name, or lack thereof

         This isn't quite true. If you name the
         daggers held, newly acquired ones that match
         otherwise but are unnamed will stack and
         acquire the existing name automatically.

         However, and often usefully, you can give
         _different_ names to daggers in the same
         stack, dropping them one by one between
         re-namings, and they will pick up unstacked.

         This can be useful when trying to create
         curse catchers, or especially when trying to
         break apart stacks for shop credit clone
         bankrupting use so that there are smaller
         cost units to sell and re-buy and re-sell --
         "small change" as it were. This works even
         if you buy and sell items by the sack-full.

         [This is a trade off, since the re-sale
         prices _round down_ from the fractional
         computation compared to buying prices (with
         a minimum of 1 except for valueless stuff),
         and so you might get less when selling them
         to the shopkeeper for the collection of
         individual unstacked daggers than for the
         daggers as a stack. The solution (there is
         an imperfect one) is left as an exercise for
         the reader.]

 >> * B/U/C status
 >> * Enchantment level
 >> * type of dagger (orcish, runed, "plain", silver, ...)

 > In addition to these real differences, the
 > identification level also matters. (At least in
 > vanilla Nethack, but I expect this to be the same
 > thing in slash'em and all other variants.) If your
 > character knows (so that you can see it in the
 > inventory listing) the enchantment level / B/U/C
 > status of one individual identical dagger and not
 > the same things of another one of the exactly same
 > kind, they don't stack together.

Yep. Thanks.

I forgot that one, another frustrating gotcha, since
that's a three way differentiation as well (unIDed,
BUC-IDed, or fully IDed).

I guess It's worth commenting then, that a "useful"
strategy for various spellings of "useful" is to
leave daggers (and other stackable missile weapons)
formally unidentified and unBUC_IDed just so that
they _will_ stack with newly acquired ones.  When
the PC has a "sufficiently large" stack of them is
soon enough to get them IDed in the
knapsack.

It is especially useful that you can f)ire cursed
daggers without getting them stuck to your hands,
and soon enough their cursed nature will make itself
evident by their behavior when thrown.

You just have to love the level of detail NetHack
contains, in between tearing your hair out because
of it -- this is what's kept the game going these
now more than two decades.

